{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>לְבִלְתִּי רוּם לְבָבוֹ מֵאֶחָיו</b> (that his heart not be raised above his brothers): The prohibition of arrogance is alluded to here in the Torah, for Scripture restrains the king from pride and loftiness of heart — how much more so all other people, who are not fitting for such things. For even regarding one who is fitting to be exalted and magnified, it warns him that his heart should be lowly like all his brothers who are lesser than he. For arrogance is a reprehensible trait and detestable before Elohim even in a king, for to HaShem alone belong greatness and exaltation, and to Him alone is praise, and in Him shall a person glory, as explained by King Solomon: תּוֹעֲבַת ה' כָּל גְּבַהּ לֵב (Proverbs 16:5) (every proud heart is an abomination to HaShem), and it is written: כִּי אִם בְּזֹאת יִתְהַלֵּל הַמִּתְהַלֵּל הַשְׂכֵּל וְיָדֹעַ אוֹתִי וגו' (Jeremiah 9:23) (but only in this shall the one who glories glory: let him understand and know Me, etc.)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:22.657922340Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}